  <title>A Cosine Sum in the Regular Heptadecagon</title>
  <dc:creator>Bruno Wang</dc:creator>
  <link>https://baindeglace.github.io/structure-fireworks/posts/cosine-sum-17/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<p>Let</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0Ax=%5Cfrac%7B2%5Cpi%7D%7B17%7D,%0A%5Cqquad%0AS=%5Ccos%20x+%5Ccos%202x+%5Ccos%204x+%5Ccos%208x.%0A"></p>
<p>The four angles appear to arise merely by repeated doubling. In fact, their exponents select exactly half of the nonzero quadratic residues modulo (17). That observation turns a trigonometric problem into a problem about grouping roots of unity.</p>
<section id="the-result" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-result">The result</h2>
<div id="prp-cosine-sum" class="theorem proposition">
<p><span class="theorem-title"><strong>Proposition 1 (The cosine sum)</strong></span> If (x=2/17), then</p>
<p><span id="eq-main"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AS=%5Ccos%20x+%5Ccos%202x+%5Ccos%204x+%5Ccos%208x%0A%20%20=%5Cfrac%7B%5Csqrt%7B17%7D-1%7D%7B4%7D.%0A%5Ctag%7B1%7D"></span></p>
</div>
<p>Before proving Proposition&nbsp;1, we express the cosines as real parts of roots of unity.</p>
</section>
<section id="roots-of-unity-and-quadratic-residues" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="roots-of-unity-and-quadratic-residues">Roots of unity and quadratic residues</h2>
<p>Set</p>
<p><span id="eq-zeta"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Czeta=e%5E%7B2%5Cpi%20i/17%7D.%0A%5Ctag%7B2%7D"></span></p>
<p>The nonzero quadratic residues modulo (17) form the set</p>
<p><span id="eq-residues"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AR=%5C%7B1,2,4,8,9,13,15,16%5C%7D,%0A%5Ctag%7B3%7D"></span></p>
<p>while the quadratic nonresidues are</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AN=%5C%7B3,5,6,7,10,11,12,14%5C%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>The set (R) is closed under negation, and (R={,,,}). Hence the Gaussian period</p>
<p><span id="eq-eta"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Ceta=%5Csum_%7Br%5Cin%20R%7D%5Czeta%5Er%0A%5Ctag%7B4%7D"></span></p>
<p>is real. Euler’s formula immediately gives</p>
<p><span id="eq-eta-s"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Ceta%0A=2%5Cbigl(%5Ccos%20x+%5Ccos%202x+%5Ccos%204x+%5Ccos%208x%5Cbigr)%0A=2S.%0A%5Ctag%7B5%7D"></span></p>
<div id="lem-period-product" class="theorem lemma">
<p><span class="theorem-title"><strong>Lemma 1 (Product of the periods)</strong></span> If</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cnu=%5Csum_%7Bn%5Cin%20N%7D%5Czeta%5En,%0A"></p>
<p>then</p>
<p><span id="eq-period-relations"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Ceta+%5Cnu=-1,%0A%5Cqquad%0A%5Ceta%5Cnu=-4.%0A%5Ctag%7B6%7D"></span></p>
</div>
<div class="proof">
<p><span class="proof-title"><em>Proof</em>. </span>The first identity follows from the sum of all nontrivial seventeenth roots of unity:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Ceta+%5Cnu=%5Csum_%7Bk=1%7D%5E%7B16%7D%5Czeta%5Ek=-1.%0A"></p>
<p>For the second, expand</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Ceta%5Cnu=%5Csum_%7Br%5Cin%20R%7D%5Csum_%7Bn%5Cin%20N%7D%5Czeta%5E%7Br+n%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>For each fixed (rR), multiplication by (r^{-1}) permutes both (R) and (N). Thus the distribution of (r+n=r(1+n/r)) is controlled by (1+N). A direct computation modulo (17) gives</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A1+N=%5C%7B4,6,7,8,11,12,13,15%5C%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>Four of these elements lie in (R), and four lie in (N). As (r) ranges over (R), every nonzero residue exponent and every nonresidue exponent therefore occurs exactly four times, while the zero exponent never occurs. Consequently,</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Ceta%5Cnu%0A=4%5Csum_%7Bk=1%7D%5E%7B16%7D%5Czeta%5Ek%0A=-4.%0A"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="proof-of-the-proposition" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="proof-of-the-proposition">Proof of the proposition</h2>
<div class="proof">
<p><span class="proof-title"><em>Proof</em>. </span>By Lemma&nbsp;1, () and () are the two roots of</p>
<p><span id="eq-period-polynomial"><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0At%5E2+t-4=0.%0A%5Ctag%7B7%7D"></span></p>
<p>Therefore</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5C%7B%5Ceta,%5Cnu%5C%7D%0A=%5Cleft%5C%7B%5Cfrac%7B-1+%5Csqrt%7B17%7D%7D2,%5Cfrac%7B-1-%5Csqrt%7B17%7D%7D2%5Cright%5C%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>It remains to determine which root is (). By Equation&nbsp;5, (=2S). Numerically—used here only to distinguish two algebraic roots that have already been determined exactly—</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AS%5Capprox%200.780776%3E0.%0A"></p>
<p>Hence</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Ceta=%5Cfrac%7B%5Csqrt%7B17%7D-1%7D%7B2%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>Dividing by (2) and applying Equation&nbsp;5 gives Equation&nbsp;1.</p>
</div>
<div id="rem-gauss-sum" class="proof remark">
<p><span class="proof-title"><em>Remark 1</em> (Remark). </span>One can instead introduce the Legendre symbol () and the quadratic Gauss sum</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AG=%5Csum_%7Ba=1%7D%5E%7B16%7D%5Cchi(a)%5Czeta%5Ea=%5Ceta-%5Cnu.%0A"></p>
<p>Since (+), we have (G=2+1). The classical Gauss-sum identity gives (G^2=17), which is equivalent to Equation&nbsp;7. The counting argument above displays the required quadratic relation directly. For a systematic account of quadratic Gauss sums, see <span class="citation" data-cites="ireland-rosen1990">Ireland and Rosen (1990)</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
